[Music]  this is your tech news briefing for  tuesday july 19th i'm zoe thomas for the  wall street journal  today marks the start of christy's fifth  annual art and tech summit where the  auction house brings together experts to  discuss the role of emerging technology  in the art market  but christie's isn't just talking about  tech's role in helping collectors buy  and sell more art on monday the world's  largest auction house announced it's  also launching a new venture capital arm  to invest in tech startups  that's despite traditional vcs pulling  back from riskier sounding tech ideas as  interest in backing some tech startups  is falling  plus the recent drop in crypto values  has raised questions about the long-term  popularity of blockchain-based art like  non-fungible tokens or nfts  so why is christie's taking this leap  now and what does it tell us about the  future of art and tech  joining us to discuss that is our art  market reporter kelly crowe  hi kelly thanks for being here thanks  for having me  so let's start with what kind of tech  companies christie's is planning on  putting its money behind  yeah so they really want to look for  companies that  are exploring questions and problems  that have long be deviled sort of the  art world but using new technology right  so they're interested in  companies that may be looking at ways to  ease the process for people to go on and  get a digital wallet and sort of spend  crypto or spend their real money on  digital things or even just sort of  look at ideas about authenticity  disputes ideas of tracking and  cataloging art as it changes owners over  time there's tons of just inefficient  ways that the art market and our world  has long tried to pay attention to how  art moves around the planet and they're  really going to be looking for companies  that are  trying to solve some of those riddles  using technology  so this isn't really about investing in  artists who are using technology or in  the kinds of tech that could be used to  build technology this is about the  collectors you know buying and selling  right  well they're interested in  infrastructure as well so you know if  you have a bunch of assets in one  particular type of cryptocurrency for  instance right they want you to be able  to go on any platform any site and just  click and buy an artwork that you like  or you know buy and spend things using  that without having to sort of have the  clunky  process of trying to move currencies  from one to the other akin to when we go  on to a website to buy an airplane  flight right the site doesn't really  care for using dollars or euros or  whatever it just kind of seamlessly sort  of converts that currency and makes it  easy for us to spend however we want to  spend it and right now that doesn't  really happen very easily in the digital  space and certainly using these new  currencies the nft  the non-fungible token world is still  sort of clunky in terms of those  transactional things have they invested  in any companies like this yet  yeah so far they've come forward as  saying that they have invested seed  capital in a company called layer zero  labs um it's a vancouver-based company  that does sort of precisely that what  it's trying to do is make it easier for  people  to spend their money across different  blockchain platforms without having to  you know spend a lot of money on what  they call  you know gas fees or these conversion  fees  okay so making it easier for people to  spend their money i mean of course  christie's as an auction house so we  would assume they'd want people to come  in and spend their money i wonder though  if you can kind of take us back a little  bit and paint a bit of a picture of how  much tech and art has been overlapping  over the last couple of years  well it's a huge departure for the  auction houses i mean these companies  christie's and sotheby's have been  around for longer than our country right  and for most of that time like the  lion's share of that time these houses  have done deals over handshakes  and sort of clubby back rooms and  lunches and i mean so much art changes  hand with a handshake and with a  contract that's signed in some cases  very little  technology at all but the pandemic  really proved to be a wake-up call  because once they couldn't get people  into the room to physically raise a  paddle right it really came home that  they needed to develop their series of  online sales they needed to be able to  put art in front of people digitally  they started looking around for  companies that could maybe create  holograms of fragile or oversized  artworks that would be tricky to ship  around the world i mean you can't really  create an edgar degas you know sculpture  amid the pandemic and send it to san  francisco and hong kong like it just  wasn't happening and so they really  started i would say out of desperation  to look for what kind of tech could  solve their own problems and then what  they found is that there's a huge  audience and a demand for that so i  would say they're kind of i mean all the  houses are a little bit johnny come  lately to this but there really are  seizing the opportunity now to catch up  i mean how much money are these auction  houses or are christie's particularly  making off of you know art that is  really much more reliant on tech  and uh you know maybe the kind of nft  boom that we've seen over the last few  years  i mean when it comes to nfts it's a  really brutal time right now to be  honest i mean christie's really got the  ball rolling when it sold that digital  collage by the artist people for 69  million dollars that really was like an  earthquake shock right throughout not  only the art world but the nft space  that collectors were willing to spend in  cryptocurrency investors were willing to  spend big bucks right for  a digital receipt attached to some  pixels i mean it really did sort of  engineer a huge revolution the problem  is that now you know crypto prices are  so volatile and crazy that the demand  for nfts sort of at the level that  christie's likes to sell them has really  shriveled for the moment so you know  last year they sold 93 million dollars  worth of nfts so far during the first  half of this year christie's has only  sold like nearly five million dollars  worth of bit of teas  so then what does this tell us i guess  long term about the art market and its  reliance or use of tech that christie's  is making this kind of investment i  think it's really fascinating because  it's really fun when you see an old  company that's  set in its ways trying to evolve right  like we need to you know adapt to  both market constraints and just sort of  the ebb and flow of technology and some  companies have long been resistant to  that and the auction houses were  resistant to that galleries were but  again because of the pandemic everyone  has had to rethink the model galleries  are hosting you know artist q as online  now and  and offering you you know virtual views  of everything they're taking to art  fairs some of this is just a natural way  in which we're moving our lives  digitally in lots of ways and so of  course we're going to move our  collecting and our buying and selling of  art online and i think that  christie's move is certainly a signal  that it doesn't want to be left out in  the cold and if new technologies are  going to end up being game changers for  all the houses they just want to make  sure you know they get a bid in early on  to be a part of it i like that get a bid  in for the auction houses all right  that's our reporter kelly crow thanks so  much for joining us kelly thanks a lot  and that's it for today's tech news  briefing  if you want more tech stories check out  our website wsj.com  and if you like our show please rate and  review it you can do that wherever you  get your podcasts  i'm zoe thomas for the wall street  journal  thanks for listening  [